Think of your immune system like a security team guarding your body. Its job is to spot and destroy invaders like viruses and bacteria. To do this effectively, it needs to recognize the specific "face" of each enemy. If it’s never seen a particular virus before, it might take too long to figure out how to fight it, leaving you sick in the meantime.

A vaccine works by showing your immune system a harmless, weakened, or fake version of that specific invader. It’s like showing a security guard a photo of a known thief before they actually break in. Your body sees this harmless "training dummy" and gets a little scared, just like it would if the real virus attacked.

In response, your immune system springs into action. It creates special soldiers called antibodies and memorizes the exact details of the invader’s shape. This process builds up a defense force and creates a memory of the enemy. Once the "training" is over and the harmless piece is cleared out, your body keeps those antibodies and memory cells on standby.

